  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Friday) \nIssued at 1239 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nIncreasing clouds and gusty south to southwest winds during the day \nTuesday as the next Pacific storm system moves east over the Great \nBasin. Guidance is in strong agreement of tracking the base of this \nupper level trough across the Four Corners region Tuesday evening \nand across the southern Rockies Wednesday morning. As a result, \nshower activity will favor western NM late Tuesday afternoon through \nearly Wednesday morning and north central NM Tuesday evening through \nlate Wednesday morning before clearing skies areawide mid to late \nWednesday. Overall impacts should be limited to some slick highways \nacross the north central mountains Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning. Some gusty southwest to west winds across northeast and \neast central NM Wednesday afternoon in the wake of the departing \nupper level trough. High temperatures on Wednesday will be slightly \nbelow average across western and north central NM due to the morning \nprecipitation and near average across central and eastern NM.\n\nSome low clouds and patchy fog development potential across the \nsoutheast plains Thursday morning due to lingering low level \nmoisture. Outside of that, mostly sunny and seasonable weather \nacross the area during the day Thursday. Ensemble cluster guidance \nis coming into better agreement on the track of another Pacific \nupper low late next week. Clusters show the upper low moving over \nnorthern CA Thursday and eastward to over the Great Basin Thursday \nnight into Friday morning before opening up into an open wave as it \nmoves across the central Rockies and scrapes northern NM during the \nday Friday. Any lower elevation rain and mountain snow shower \nactivity looks to favor areas along and north of I-40 with gusty \nsouth to west-southwest winds for all areas at this time. The \nPacific front with this system will cool high temperatures slightly \nbelow average across western NM Friday. Clearing skies Friday night \ninto Saturday morning in the wake of this storm system with slightly \nbelow average temperatures across western and central NM and near \naverage temperatures across eastern NM.",
